d['assignment']='Stevens has the minimum eligible count, one, and is selected. His Lonchar opinion supplies the need for a genuine statutory dismissal predicate, while his Stewart join and complete present commitment support the distinction between a prisoner’s first chosen motion and a judicially imposed label. That concrete combination fits the jurisdiction, supervised conversion rule and preserved earlier judgment. Breyer, at two, is the strongest alternative: his recorded uniform-process rationale and complete joins would carry the same opinion. His practical implementation advantage is not sufficient to displace Stevens’s lower count and direct authorship of the governing predicate principle. The Chief is fully eligible but at three lies outside this minimum-plus-one pool; no exception is invoked. Scalia writes the common merits concurrence because its central party-choice and statutory-predicate objection is explicitly his frozen ground and Thomas adopts that same ground; this separate writing is excluded from lead counts.'
